messages:
devon: Quick question — deploying the umbrella chart to app-qa, but the metrics-collector subchart expects a Secret in its own namespace. We actually keep the secret in shared-secrets namespace (centralized). What's the least invasive way to make the subchart pick up the secret from shared-secrets without forking the whole subchart?

deploy-bot: CI lint failed on metrics-collector: templates/deployment.yaml: envFrom.secretRef.name: not found in values (helm lint) :thinking_face:

amanda: Two common patterns: 1) pass a namespace override into the subchart via the umbrella values (e.g. metrics.secretNamespace: shared-secrets) and change the subchart template to default to that; 2) use the `lookup` function to check for the Secret at render time and conditionally render envFrom. Both have tradeoffs.

kai: fwiw, `lookup` requires the user running helm to have RBAC read access to Secrets in the target ns. If we can't guarantee that, better to avoid cluster lookups in templates. Using a values override is simpler and doesn't require extra permissions.

olga: example helper I slapped together if we go the `values override` route — put this in metrics/templates/_helpers.tpl and use it in the Deployment: 

```tpl
{{- define "metrics.secretNamespace" -}}
{{- if .Values.secretNamespace }}
{{-   .Values.secretNamespace | quote -}}
{{- else -}}
{{-   .Release.Namespace | quote -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
```

Then in metrics/templates/deployment.yaml: 

```yaml
envFrom:
  - secretRef:
      name: {{ .Values.secretName }}
      namespace: {{ include "metrics.secretNamespace" . }}
```

olga: Note: kubernetes API actually ignores `namespace` on secretRef inside envFrom (Secret refs are namespaced by the pod's namespace). You can't reference a secret in another namespace via envFrom/secretRef in the Pod spec. So the above will NOT work if you actually need cross-namespace mount. I should've caught that earlier :facepalm:

devon: Oh — right. I forgot that secretRef namespace field isn't valid for envFrom. Thanks for the callout. So our options are narrowing: sync the secret into the app namespace (ExternalSecret / operator), mount it via projected volume from a service that can pull it, or make the subchart accept the secret value as a value (not ideal).

amanda: Exactly. For cross-namespace secrets you either: 1) copy/sync the secret into the pod namespace (ExternalSecrets, sealed-secrets sync), 2) inject via values (umbrella chart sets metrics.secretData: {KEY: val}) during CI/deploy (but secret in values is sensitive), or 3) change metrics to read from a ClusterSecret controller (not common). My vote: use ExternalSecrets to sync shared-secrets -> app-qa, document it, and add a small helmsync checklist.

kai: If we go ExternalSecrets, add a step in our deploy pipeline to ensure sync CR exists in the app namespace and add a quick `kubectl get secret -n app-qa <name>` check in CI before helm upgrade. That avoids RBAC assumptions at template render time.

olga: If you want a defensible template change (no lookups), we can make the subchart tolerate absence and won't crash: 

```yaml
env:
{{- if .Values.secretName }}
  - name: METRIC_KEY
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: {{ .Values.secretName }}
        key: api_key
{{- else }}
  - name: METRIC_KEY
    value: ""
{{- end }}
```

devon: OK — plan: I'll add an ExternalSecret CR in app-qa that syncs from shared-secrets (team infra will review) and update the metrics values docs to recommend `secretName` be set (and show the tolerant template). I'll also open a tiny PR to make metrics handle missing secrets gracefully (per olga snippet).

amanda: Perfect. Can you include a note in the umbrella values doc about why we chose sync vs lookup? Helps future debuggers. :memo:

devon: Will do. I'll ping when PR is up. Thanks all! :rocket:
